<speak>
<p>We now finish the general argument. Node seven gave an alternating sum, script S, congruent to zero modulo one plus i. Node five gave E of P congruent to the all ones polynomial, J of P. We will evaluate that same sum a second way.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Let I contain the distinct primes dividing our odd integer u, greater than one. Each subset S supplies the product of its chosen primitive roots. The empty subset gives one. Every nonempty subset gives a nontrivial root whose order divides u squared. Therefore the u squared terms of J cancel exactly at every nonempty input. At one, they add to u squared. These are exact geometric sums, before reduction.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Now reduce modulo one plus i. We may replace each E evaluation by its corresponding J evaluation. The denominators stay invertible: the norm identity supplies their inverses after inverting the odd number u. Thus a zero numerator really does give a zero term. Every nonempty subset disappears, leaving only the empty subset.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>The surviving term is J of one divided by c of one. Since c of one is positive or negative u, this quotient is positive or negative u. The integer u is odd. Pairs of ones vanish modulo one plus i, leaving one; a negative sign has the same residue. So script S is congruent to one.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Compare the two conclusions. Node seven gives residue zero, while the all ones polynomial gives residue one for the same sum. Our reduction ring is nonzero, so zero and one are different. This contradiction rules out every odd u greater than one.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Return to the order restriction. An order n greater than one must equal four u squared, with u odd. We have eliminated every u greater than one. The only remaining value is u equal to one, giving order four. Order one is the separate case.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Both remaining orders occur. At order one, use the one by one matrix with entry one. At order four, use our circulant example whose first row is negative one, one, one, one. Its rows are mutually orthogonal and have squared length four. The argument concludes that a real circulant Hadamard matrix exists exactly at orders one and four. This completes the eight step proof map.<break time="1200ms"/></p>
</speak>
